@startuml
title Use case diagram — local two-player chess (as implemented)

left to right direction
actor "Human player\n(White or Black, same machine)" as P

rectangle "Chess application (pygame window)" {
  usecase "Start / restart game" as UC1
  usecase "Select own piece" as UC2
  usecase "View legal targets" as UC3
  usecase "Make a move" as UC4
  usecase "Castle" as UC5
  usecase "Capture (incl. en passant)" as UC6
  usecase "Auto-promote pawn to queen" as UC7
  usecase "View move history" as UC8
  usecase "View captured pieces" as UC9
  usecase "See check / game-over overlay" as UC10
  usecase "Quit window" as UC11
}

P --> UC1
P --> UC2
P --> UC4
P --> UC8
P --> UC9
P --> UC10
P --> UC11

UC2 .> UC3 : <<include>>
UC4 .> UC2 : <<include>>
UC5 .> UC4 : <<extend>>
UC6 .> UC4 : <<extend>>
UC7 .> UC4 : <<extend>>

note bottom of UC1
  Launching main.py creates Board() immediately.
  The "New Game" button also constructs Board().
  No mode picker, login, or confirmation dialog.
end note

note bottom of UC7
  Always Queen; the player cannot choose
  knight / bishop / rook.
end note
@enduml
